WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.306
Where does the data of a FundsXML file live once it has arrived?

2
00:00:04.356 --> 00:00:15.994
In this film you see two ways of storing FundsXML in a database, a table model for the core data, the traps around keys and currencies, and how to keep the history of deliveries and corrections.

3
00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:18.949
There are two basic ways.

4
00:00:18.999 --> 00:00:22.811
The first: store the whole document in an X M L column.

5
00:00:22.861 --> 00:00:28.568
PostgreSQL, Oracle and SQL Server all have one, and they can query it with X Path.

6
00:00:28.618 --> 00:00:33.342
The second: shred the document into tables, one per business object.

7
00:00:33.392 --> 00:00:40.012
Most setups do both: they keep the original file, and they load the data they need into tables.

8
00:00:41.362 --> 00:00:46.246
Storing the document is simple and keeps everything, also data you do not use today.

9
00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:53.765
Here the deliveries table holds every file in an X M L column, and a query reads the total net asset value directly with X Path.

10
00:00:53.815 --> 00:00:59.815
That is good for archives and for occasional questions, but slow for reporting over thousands of files.

11
00:01:01.165 --> 00:01:06.920
Some teams convert the file to J S O N and store that instead, because web applications like it.

12
00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:14.116
That works, but the conversion loses details: the order of elements and the difference between attributes and elements.

13
00:01:14.166 --> 00:01:20.465
Whatever you choose for daily use, keep the original file, unchanged, as the record of what was received.

14
00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:24.408
Many setups arrange the database in layers.

15
00:01:24.458 --> 00:01:26.805
The raw layer holds the original files.

16
00:01:26.855 --> 00:01:30.677
The staging layer holds the data as delivered, still unchecked.

17
00:01:30.727 --> 00:01:34.856
The core layer holds checked data with the own keys and the history.

18
00:01:34.906 --> 00:01:39.896
And the reporting layer offers views for the people and systems that use the data.

19
00:01:39.946 --> 00:01:44.198
Each layer has one job, and errors stay in the layer where they occur.

20
00:01:45.548 --> 00:01:47.738
For daily work, tables are better.

21
00:01:47.788 --> 00:01:51.295
The reference model follows the structure of FundsXML.

22
00:01:51.345 --> 00:01:53.600
Every delivery is registered first.

23
00:01:53.650 --> 00:02:05.984
Then come funds and, for umbrellas, their subfunds; share classes with their prices and net asset values; portfolios with their positions; the assets the positions refer to; and documents.

24
00:02:07.334 --> 00:02:09.114
Here is the table definition.

25
00:02:09.164 --> 00:02:20.662
The columns follow the FundsXML elements, so that the mapping stays easy to read: the fund has its LEI and its name, the share class its ISIN, its currency and the fund it belongs to.

26
00:02:20.712 --> 00:02:25.206
A price row has the share class, the date, the price type and the value.

27
00:02:26.556 --> 00:02:29.052
Here is one position on its way into a table.

28
00:02:29.102 --> 00:02:34.880
On the left in the file: the reference to the asset, the total value with its currency, and the weight.

29
00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:46.367
On the right the row: the asset is no longer the reference of the file, but the key of the database; the value keeps its currency; and the row knows the portfolio, the date and the delivery it came from.

30
00:02:47.717 --> 00:02:50.736
One structural point: single funds and umbrellas.

31
00:02:50.786 --> 00:02:59.128
FundsXML says with the single fund flag whether a fund has share classes directly, or subfunds that each have their own share classes.

32
00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:07.958
In the database, one fund table with a parent column handles both cases, so that queries do not need to know which kind of fund they look at.

33
00:03:09.308 --> 00:03:10.757
One trap concerns keys.

34
00:03:10.807 --> 00:03:14.213
A position points to its asset through the Unique ID.

35
00:03:14.263 --> 00:03:20.863
But that ID is only valid inside one file: the next delivery may use the same ID for another security.

36
00:03:20.913 --> 00:03:31.033
So the database needs its own keys, based on the ISIN, the LEI or the sender's own codes, and the link between position and asset is resolved while loading.

37
00:03:32.383 --> 00:03:34.426
And not every asset has an ISIN.

38
00:03:34.476 --> 00:03:39.921
Cash accounts, over-the-counter derivatives and currency forwards usually have none.

39
00:03:39.971 --> 00:03:45.743
For them, build a key from the sender, the type of asset and the sender's own identifier.

40
00:03:45.793 --> 00:03:55.489
Keep all identifiers of an asset in a table of their own: ISIN, WKN, Bloomberg ticker and internal codes, so that any of them can be used to find it.

41
00:03:56.839 --> 00:03:58.320
Amounts are the next trap.

42
00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:05.975
In FundsXML, one amount can be given in several currencies at once, with flags for the fund currency and the share-class currency.

43
00:04:06.025 --> 00:04:11.451
So the database stores the currency with every amount, and if needed several rows per amount.

44
00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:16.044
A bare number without its currency is the most common source of wrong reports.

45
00:04:17.394 --> 00:04:24.210
Many FundsXML elements use fixed lists of codes: document types, price types, data operations and many more.

46
00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:30.122
Store them in lookup tables, filled from the schema, and refresh them with every new version.

47
00:04:30.172 --> 00:04:35.208
Then the database can check incoming codes, and reports can show readable names.

48
00:04:36.558 --> 00:04:37.799
Dates need care too.

49
00:04:37.849 --> 00:04:44.321
A file has a content date, the day its data is valid for, and a generation time, when it was written.

50
00:04:44.371 --> 00:04:47.163
A price has its own date, the valuation day.

51
00:04:47.213 --> 00:04:52.458
These can all differ: a file generated on Monday morning carries prices of Friday.

52
00:04:52.508 --> 00:04:56.655
Store all of them, and decide clearly which one your reports use.

53
00:04:58.005 --> 00:05:00.305
Data changes, and corrections arrive.

54
00:05:00.355 --> 00:05:08.370
So every row remembers where it came from: the delivery, its document ID, and the content date, the date the data is valid for.

55
00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:14.720
A delivery with the data operation amend replaces the values of an earlier one; delete removes them.

56
00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:18.975
The original rows are not overwritten, they are marked as replaced.

57
00:05:20.325 --> 00:05:30.003
This makes questions like these simple: what was the net asset value of this share class on the thirty-first of October, as known today, and as known on the first of November?

58
00:05:30.053 --> 00:05:32.504
Here the second delivery corrected one value.

59
00:05:32.554 --> 00:05:36.618
Both versions are in the table, and the query picks the one that is valid.

60
00:05:37.968 --> 00:05:41.022
Users should not have to know how the history works.

61
00:05:41.072 --> 00:05:46.991
Offer views for each use: the current prices, the latest portfolio per fund, the documents in force.

62
00:05:47.041 --> 00:05:52.362
The views hide the logic of deliveries and replaced rows, and give consumers stable names.

63
00:05:52.412 --> 00:05:57.615
Then you can change the tables underneath without breaking every report that reads them.

64
00:05:58.965 --> 00:06:01.646
Store the quality of the data with the data.

65
00:06:01.696 --> 00:06:09.033
Each delivery has a status, and rows can carry warnings from the checks: an unusual price change, a missing identifier.

66
00:06:09.083 --> 00:06:12.446
The database records who released a delivery, and when.

67
00:06:12.496 --> 00:06:17.288
Reports read only released data, and anyone can see why a value was held back.

68
00:06:18.638 --> 00:06:20.301
Portfolios are confidential.

69
00:06:20.351 --> 00:06:27.762
A data vendor or an insurer may hold the portfolios of many clients in one database, and each client may only see its own.

70
00:06:27.812 --> 00:06:35.836
So control access per client or per fund, separate the roles of loading, releasing and reading, and keep a log of who accessed what.

71
00:06:35.886 --> 00:06:39.016
Most databases support this with row-level security.

72
00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:47.308
Regulatory templates are a world of their own: hundreds of fields each, and a new version every few years.

73
00:06:47.358 --> 00:06:48.957
Two approaches work well.

74
00:06:49.007 --> 00:06:54.235
Keep each template in an X M L column and extract only the fields you report on.

75
00:06:54.285 --> 00:06:56.939
Or create one table per template version.

76
00:06:56.989 --> 00:07:00.963
Avoid one giant table for all versions; it ends in confusion.

77
00:07:02.313 --> 00:07:04.170
A few words on performance.

78
00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:07.913
Positions grow fastest: thousands per fund and month.

79
00:07:07.963 --> 00:07:11.938
Index the columns you search by: ISIN, date and delivery.

80
00:07:11.988 --> 00:07:14.198
Partition large tables by month.

81
00:07:14.248 --> 00:07:16.952
And load in bulk instead of row by row.

82
00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:23.802
With these measures, ordinary databases handle many years of FundsXML deliveries without trouble.

83
00:07:25.152 --> 00:07:28.700
A last piece of advice: do not model the whole schema at once.

84
00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:35.026
Start with the data you really use, keep the original file next to it, and add tables when new needs come up.

85
00:07:35.076 --> 00:07:39.785
The schema has thousands of elements, and most companies use a small part of them.

86
00:07:41.135 --> 00:07:47.421
That was FundsXML in a database: documents and tables, keys, amounts and codes, and history.

87
00:07:47.471 --> 00:07:51.210
In the next film, you load large files into this model.

88
00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:52.587
Thanks for watching.
